{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-88cf00a88738a81a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tDO MUSIC FOUNDATION MODELS\n2\tEMBED PITCH IN HELICAL STRUCTURE?\n3\tHayato Yagi∗,1\n4\tShinnosuke Takamichi∗,1,2\n5\tRin Sato3\n6\tKeitaro Tanaka3\n7\tShigeo Morishima3\n8\t1\n9\t2\n10\t3\n11\tKeio University, Japan\n12\tThe University of Tokyo, Japan\n13\tWaseda University, Japan\n14\t∗\n15\t\n16\t{hayatobuti523,shinnosuke_takamichi}@keio.jp\n17\t\n18\t1. INTRODUCTION\n19\tFoundation models have recently advanced music information processing, following their success in natural language\n20\tprocessing (NLP). In particular, self-supervised learning\n21\t(SSL) on large-scale music data has produced powerful\n22\tmusic-specific models, known as music foundation models (MFMs) [1–5]. MFMs exhibit strong versatility in both\n23\tmusic generation and understanding tasks such as classification and analysis, drawing attention for their potential to\n24\tsupport new forms of music creation [5, 6].\n25\tDespite their impressive capabilities, the internal mechanisms of MFMs remain unclear. In particular, how they\n26\tacquire and use musical knowledge such as pitch and harmony is not well understood. To address this, recent studies have analyzed intermediate representations learned by\n27\tMFMs, using probing methods to implicitly evaluate what\n28\tmusical knowledge is encoded [7, 8]. Trained on humancomposed music, MFMs may encode such knowledge as\n29\tlatent structures. However, it still remains unclear how\n30\tsuch knowledge is explicitly represented as a geometric\n31\tstructure. Clarifying this would provide important insights into whether MFMs understand musical knowledge\n32\tas structured representations.\n33\tAs a first step toward understanding the internal geometry of MFMs, we focus on pitch, a fundamental musi© H. Yagi, S. Takamichi, R. Sato, K. Tanaka, and S. Morishima. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\n34\tLicense (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: H. Yagi, S. Takamichi, R. Sato, K.\n35\tTanaka, and S. Morishima, “Do Music Foundation Models Embed Pitch\n36\tin Helical Structure?”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n37\t\n38\tInstruments\n39\t\n40\tFoundation model\n41\t\n42\tSingle note\n43\t\n44\t× 𝑁key\n45\t\n46\tArtificial test signal\n47\tflat\n48\t𝑓0 2𝑓0 3𝑓0 4𝑓0\n49\t\n50\t2. Extraction\n51\t\n52\tdecay\n53\t𝑓0 2𝑓0 3𝑓0 4𝑓0\n54\t\n55\t…\n56\t\n57\t3. Evaluation\n58\t3D features\n59\t\n60\tTransformer\n61\tTransformer\n62\tTransformer\n63\t\n64\tThis study analyzes the intermediate representations of\n65\tmusic foundation models (MFMs) and reports the geometric structures used to represent pitch information. By inputting isolated musical notes into trained MFMs and analyzing their principal components, we reveal that the representations form a helical structure reflecting the octave\n66\tperiodicity of pitch. Furthermore, we show that the clarity\n67\tand geometry of this helical structure vary not only across\n68\tmodels but also with the acoustic properties of the input\n69\tsignals. Our analysis provides a novel approach for clarifying the internal mechanisms of MFMs.\n70\t\n71\t1. Input: Various timbres\n72\t\n73\tTransformer\n74\tTransformer\n75\t\n76\tarXiv:2607.29086v1 [cs.SD] 31 Jul 2026\n77\t\n78\tABSTRACT\n79\t\n80\tPCA\n81\tFitting\n82\t\n83\tExtract\n84\tIntermediate\n85\trepresentation\n86\t\n87\tHelicality\n88\t\n89\tFigure 1: Overview. We extract pitch-dependent features\n90\tfrom a music foundation model and evaluate their helical\n91\tstructure using PCA and parametric fitting.\n92\t\n93\tcal element. In music psychology, pitch has long been\n94\tmodeled as a helical structure combining pitch height and\n95\toctave periodicity [9]. We hypothesize that MFMs may\n96\texhibit this structure and investigate whether their intermediate representations form a pitch helix. We extract\n97\tpitch-related features from the representations and quantify the degree to which they follow a helical structure using a dedicated metric (Fig. 1). In this study, we analyze\n98\ttwo generative MFMs and show that (1) the helical structure emerges across a wide range of instrument sounds,\n99\tand (2) this structure is primarily driven by specific harmonics, as revealed by experiments with artificial test signals. Our code is available at https://github.com/\n100\ttakamichi-lab/mfm-pitch-helix.\n101\t2. RELATED WORK\n102\t2.1 Interpretability of Foundation Models\n103\tUnderstanding the intermediate representations of foundation models has become a key research topic, especially in\n104\tNLP. Prior work shows that models like GPT-2 [10] encode\n105\tperiodic concepts (e.g., days, months) as circular geometric patterns [11], suggesting they form human-like conceptual structures beyond simple statistical patterns [12–15].\n106\tIn music information processing, probing [16] has been\n107\twidely used to assess whether intermediate representations\n108\tencode musical attributes by measuring how well a linear classifier predicts them. It has been applied to MFMs\n109\tsuch as Jukebox [1] and MERT [4], demonstrating that in-\n110\t\n111\t\fheight\n112\t\n113\tAcoustic grand piano\n114\t\n115\tViolin\n116\t\n117\tRecorder\n118\t\n119\tSawtooth wave lead\n120\t\n121\tHammond organ\n122\t\n123\tAcoustic steel guitar\n124\t\n125\tC4\n126\tC3\n127\tG F# F\n128\tE\n129\tAG#\n130\tA# B class DD#\n131\tC C#\n132\tFigure 2: Shepard’s pitch helix [9]. Vertical height and\n133\tcircular angle correspond to pitch height and pitch class,\n134\trespectively.\n135\t\n136\ttermediate layers encode musical attributes such as pitch,\n137\tchords, tempo, and genres [4, 5, 7, 8, 17]. Some of the studies [7, 8] also report that deeper layers better encode pitch\n138\tand chords. However, these studies primarily assess what\n139\tmusical attributes are encoded in the intermediate representations via probe performance. Thus, unlike in NLP,\n140\tresearch that directly examines how music-theoretical concepts are represented as geometric structures in MFMs remains limited.\n141\t2.2 Pitch Helix\n142\tIn music psychology, pitch is conceptualized along two\n143\tperceptual dimensions: pitch height, corresponding to absolute frequency, and pitch class, representing note identity\n144\twithin the twelve-tone octave cycle (e.g., C, C♯, D), regardless of octave. This dual representation leads to the principle of octave equivalence, where tones that are one octave\n145\tapart are perceived as similar. To capture this property,\n146\tthe pitch helix has been proposed as a model that arranges\n147\tpitches in a three-dimensional spiral [9]. In this structure,\n148\tvertical position reflects pitch height, while the angular position represents pitch class, resulting in tones recurring at\n149\tthe same angular position in every octave (Fig. 2).\n150\tRecent work shows that such helical structures can\n151\temerge from raw music signals without supervision [18].\n152\tHowever, it lacks quantitative evaluation. To overcome\n153\tthis, a metric called Helicality has been proposed to measure how closely three-dimensional points fit to an ideal\n154\thelix [19]. In our study, we apply the Helicality to the intermediate representations of MFMs in order to determine\n155\twhether they encode pitch in a helical form.\n156\t2.3 Timbre and Harmonic Structure\n157\tInstrument sounds are complex tones composed of a fundamental frequency f0 and multiple harmonics. The frequencies, amplitudes, and temporal evolution of these\n158\tcomponents determine timbre. The harmonic structure depends on the sound production mechanism and physical\n159\tproperties of the vibrating body, and varies across instrument types such as string, wind, and percussion [20].\n160\tAs shown in Fig. 3, even for the same pitch (C4), amplitude distributions around f0 and harmonic components\n161\tvary across instruments. Such differences likely influence\n162\tthe internal pitch representations learned by MFMs.\n163\t\n164\tFigure 3: Amplitude spectra for various instruments. The\n165\tred dashed line indicates the fundamental frequency f0 ,\n166\tand the black dashed lines indicate subharmonics f0 /h or\n167\tharmonics hf0 , where h is an integer.\n168\t\n169\t2.4 Music Foundation Models\n170\tWe focus on two generative MFMs used in our experiments: Jukebox [1] and MusicGen [2], both pretrained on\n171\tlarge-scale music data via SSL.\n172\tJukebox. Jukebox uses a vector quantized variational autoencoder [21] to convert music signals into discrete tokens, which are then autoregressively predicted by a hierarchical Transformer [22] decoder. The decoder consists\n173\tof three levels: the top captures long-term musical features,\n174\twhile the middle and bottom refine temporal resolution.\n175\tMusicGen. MusicGen is a non-hierarchical generative\n176\tmodel consisting of a pretrained neural audio codec [23],\n177\ta T5 text encoder [24], and a Transformer decoder.\n178\t\n179\t3. METHOD\n180\t3.1 Extracting Pitch-Dependent Features\n181\tAs shown in Fig. 1, we input monophonic music signals with specific pitches into the model’s pretrained audio tokenizer and pass the resulting token sequences to the\n182\tTransformer decoder. Following prior work [7], we obtain a time series of intermediate representations from each\n183\tTransformer-decoder layer and average them over time to\n184\tobtain one vector per isolated musical note. Repeating this\n185\tprocess for Nkey pitches yields a set of pitch-conditioned\n186\tintermediate representation vectors for each layer.\n187\tTo capture pitch-related structure, we apply principal\n188\tcomponent analysis (PCA) to the set of pitch-conditioned\n189\tintermediate representations obtained from each layer. We\n190\tcompute \u0001the top five principal components and enumerate all 53 = 10 combinations of three components. For\n191\teach combination, the pitch-conditioned representations\n192\tare projected into a three-dimensional space, resulting\n193\tin ten different sets of three-dimensional embeddings for\n194\teach layer. These embeddings are then used to evaluate\n195\tthe degree to which each layer encodes a helical structure\n196\twith respect to pitch.\n197\t\n198\t\fchroma\n199\tchroma\n200\t\n201\t00\n202\t\n203\t𝜃(𝑡)\n204\t==\n205\t𝜔chroma\n206\t(𝑡 −\n207\t𝑡0 )𝑡0 )\n208\t𝜃(𝑡)\n209\t𝜔chroma\n210\t(𝑡 −\n211\t\n212\t𝜃(𝑡)\n213\t= =\n214\t𝜔chroma\n215\tℎ(𝑡)\n216\tℎpitch (𝑡\n217\t∙ 𝑡−\n218\t+ 𝑡ℎ00)\n219\t𝑟(𝑡) = 𝑟slope ∙ 𝑡 + 𝑟0\n220\t𝜃(𝑡) = 𝜔chroma (𝑡 − 𝑡0 )\n221\t𝒙𝑡\n222\t𝒚(𝑡)\n223\t𝒙\n224\t𝒚(𝑡)𝒚(𝑡)\n225\tℎ𝑡pitch\n226\t𝒙𝒙𝑡𝑡 = 0.1 𝒚(𝑡)\n227\t𝒙\n228\t𝒚(𝑡)\n229\t𝒙\n230\t𝒚(𝑡)\n231\t𝑡\n232\t𝑡\n233\tℎ\n234\t= 0.2\n235\t\n236\tℎpitch = 0.1\n237\t==\n238\t0.1\n239\tℎℎpitch\n240\t(a)ℎpitch\n241\tℎpitch\n242\t=0.2\n243\t0.1\n244\t0.1\n245\tpitch =\n246\t\n247\tZ\n248\t\n249\t0.1\n250\tℎℎpitch\n251\t===\n252\t0.2\n253\t=\n254\t0.1\n255\tpitch\n256\t𝑟slope\n257\tpitch\n258\tℎpitch\n259\t=0.1\n260\t0.2\n261\tℎℎ\n262\t0.2\n263\tpitch =\n264\t0.2\n265\t𝑟ℎ\n266\t===\n267\t0.1\n268\tℎ\n269\t=\n270\t0.2\n271\tpitch\n272\t𝑟slope\n273\t0.0\n274\tslope\n275\tpitch\n276\t𝑟\n277\t=\n278\t0.1\n279\t𝑟\n280\t= 0.1\n281\tslope\n282\tslope\n283\t\n284\t0.0\n285\t𝑟𝑟slope\n286\t==−0.1\n287\t=\n288\t0.0\n289\tslope\n290\tslope\n291\t𝜔𝑟𝑟𝑟chroma\n292\t𝜋/3\n293\t==−0.1\n294\t−0.1\n295\tslope =\n296\tslope\n297\t=\n298\t−0.1\n299\t𝑟\n300\t−0.1\n301\t𝜔𝑟slope\n302\t=\n303\t𝜋/3\n304\tslope ==\n305\t𝜔𝜔\n306\t𝜋/6\n307\tchroma\n308\tchroma\n309\t𝜔\n310\t=\n311\t𝜋/3\n312\t=\n313\t𝜋/3\n314\tchroma\n315\tchroma\n316\t𝜋/3\n317\t(c)𝜔𝜔chroma\n318\t==𝜋/6\n319\t𝜔\n320\t=\n321\t𝜋/3\n322\tchroma\n323\t𝜔chroma\n324\t= 𝜋/6\n325\t𝜋/6\n326\t𝜔\n327\t=\n328\tchroma\n329\tchroma\n330\t\n331\t𝜔chroma\n332\t= 𝜋/6\n333\t𝜔chroma\n334\t= 𝜋/6\n335\t\n336\t𝒙𝑡\n337\t\n338\tZ\n339\t\n340\t𝒚(𝑡)\n341\t\n342\tℋ =\n343\t\n344\t𝒚(𝑡)\n345\t\n346\tℎpitch\n347\t𝑟 = 0.1\n348\t= 0.1\n349\t\n350\t1 1\n351\t, , 1, 2, 4, 8\n352\t4 2\n353\t\n354\tℋ = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\n355\t\n356\tslope\n357\t\n358\tℎpitch\n359\t𝑟 = 0.2\n360\t= 0.0\n361\t\n362\t𝒙𝑡\n363\t\n364\tslope\n365\t\n366\tslope\n367\tslope\n368\t\n369\t0.1\n370\t==0.0\n371\t=\n372\t0.1\n373\tslope\n374\tslope\n375\t(b)𝑟𝑟\n376\t𝑟slope\n377\t=−0.1\n378\t0.0\n379\t𝑟𝑟𝑟slope==\n380\t0.0\n381\t\n382\tpitch\n383\t\n384\t𝒙𝑡\n385\t\n386\tZ\n387\t\n388\t𝑟slope\n389\t= 0.1\n390\tℎ\n391\t= −0.1\n392\t0.1\n393\t𝑟 pitch\n394\t=\n395\tslope\n396\t\n397\t𝒚(𝑡)\n398\t\n399\tZ\n400\t\n401\t𝑟slope\n402\t= 0.0\n403\tℎpitch\n404\t= 0.2\n405\t𝜔\n406\tchroma = 𝜋/3\n407\t𝑟slope\n408\t= −0.1\n409\t𝑟slope\n410\t= 0.1\n411\t𝜔\n412\tchroma = 𝜋/6\n413\tZ\n414\t\n415\t𝜔chroma\n416\t𝑟slope = 𝜋/3\n417\t0.0\n418\t𝜔chroma\n419\t𝑟slope = 𝜋/6\n420\t−0.1\n421\tX\n422\t\n423\tZ\n424\t\n425\tX\n426\t\n427\t𝜔chroma in\n428\t= 𝜋/3\n429\tFigure 4: Effects of parameters\n430\tEq. (1). (a) hpitch\n431\t𝜔\n432\t= 𝜋/6\n433\tcontrols vertical spacing, (b) rchroma\n434\tslope affects conicity (positive: expanding, negative: contracting), (c) ωchroma sets\n435\tthe number of pitches per rotation. Each column shows a\n436\t3D view (left) and its xz-plane projection (right).\n437\t\n438\t3.2 Parametric Pitch Helix Model\n439\tWe model the three-dimensional embeddings using a parametric helical function defined by nine parameters: the\n440\theight offset h0 , height slope hpitch , radius offset r0 , radius\n441\tslope rslope , angular frequency ωchroma , phase offset p0 ,\n442\tand three orthonormal basis vectors c, u, v ∈ R3 . Here, c\n443\trepresents the central axis of the helix, while u and v span\n444\tthe rotation plane. These parameters control the position,\n445\tshape, and pitch-wise rotation of the helix.\n446\tUsing these parameters, the helix function y(p) for the\n447\tsemitone-ordered pitch index p = 1, . . . , Nkey is given by:\n448\ty(p) = h(p) · c + r(p) (cos θ(p) · u + sin θ(p) · v)\n449\t\n450\t\n451\th(p) = hpitch · p + h0\n452\twhere r(p) = rslope · p + r0\n453\t(1)\n454\t\n455\t\n456\tθ(p) = ωchroma · (p − p0 ).\n457\th(p), r(p), and θ(p) denote the height, radius, and phase,\n458\trespectively. They are linear functions of p (Fig. 4). While\n459\tthe linear increase of h(p) and θ(p) follows Shepard’s pitch\n460\thelix (Fig. 2), r(p) is also defined linearly to capture the\n461\tcone-like variations observed in our experiments. The parameters of y(p) are optimized within predefined search\n462\tranges to minimize the mean squared error (MSE) with respect to the three-dimensional embeddings.\n463\t\n464\tFigure 5: Amplitude spectra for different H with a fixed\n465\tf0 and decay tilt.\n466\t𝑑 = decay\n467\t\n468\tFigure 6: Amplitude spectra for different tilt conditions\n469\twith a fixed f0 and H.\n470\t\n471\t3.4 Analysis Using Artificial Test Signals\n472\tAs described in Sec. 2.3, the presence and amplitude of\n473\tharmonic components in instrument sounds affect timbre.\n474\tIn this section, we describe a methodology for investigating which signal components contribute to helix formation\n475\tby measuring Helicality with artificial test signals.\n476\t3.4.1 Synthesis of Harmonic-Controlled Artificial Signals\n477\t(d)\n478\t\n479\tAn artificial signal sH,f0 (t) at time t is defined as:\n480\tX (d)\n481\t(d)\n482\tah sin(2πf0 ht) ,\n483\tsH,f0 (t) =\n484\t\n485\tTo quantify how well the three-dimensional embeddings\n486\talign with the helical model in Eq. (1), we apply the Helicality [19] as the inverse of the MSE. A higher score indicates a closer fit to the helical structure.\n487\tFormally,\n488\tgiven three-dimensional embeddings\n489\tNkey\n490\t{xp }p=1\n491\t, the Helicality is defined as:\n492\t\n493\t−1\n494\tNkey\n495\tX\n496\t1\n497\t2\n498\tHelicality = \n499\t|xp − y(p)|  . (2)\n500\tNkey p=1\n501\t\u0001\n502\tWe compute this score for each of the 53 projections described in Sec. 3.1, and use the maximum score as the Helicality for each layer.\n503\t\n504\t(3)\n505\t\n506\th∈H\n507\t\n508\twhere H denotes the set of harmonic indices included in\n509\tthe signal. The fundamental component h = 1 is always\n510\tincluded in H, while integer harmonics h = 2, 3, . . . and\n511\tsubharmonics h = 21 , 13 , . . . are selectively included. Let\n512\tHall denote the set of all possible harmonic indices, i.e.,\n513\tH ⊂ Hall . The effects of different H are shown in Fig. 5.\n514\tA variable d denotes the spectral tilt, where d ∈ D =\n515\t{flat, decay}. To control the amplitude of each harmonic\n516\t(d)\n517\tcomponent, we define the amplitude coefficients ah as:\n518\t(flat)\n519\t\n520\tah\n521\t\n522\t= 1,\n523\t\n524\t(4)\n525\t\n526\t(decay)\n527\t\n528\t= min(h, 1/h).\n529\t\n530\t(5)\n531\t\n532\tah\n533\t3.3 Helicality\n534\t\n535\t𝑑 = flat\n536\t\n537\tEquation (4) corresponds to equal-amplitude harmonics,\n538\twhereas Eq. (5) applies symmetric amplitude decay as the\n539\tfrequency ratio deviates from f0 (Fig. 6). These conditions\n540\tyield different timbres for the same harmonic index h.\n541\t3.4.2 Helicality for Each Condition\n542\tFor each harmonic condition (H, d), we synthesize Nkey\n543\tsignals with different pitches. We denote the set of the\n544\t(d)\n545\t(d)\n546\tsignals as SH = {sH,f0 }f0 ∈F , where F is the set of fundamental frequencies corresponding to a predefined pitch\n547\trange. The signals are input into a target MFM, and Helicality is computed for each layer as described in Sec. 3.3.\n548\tFor the statistical analysis, we consider two dependent\n549\tvariables that summarize the layer-wise distribution of He(d)\n550\t(d)\n551\tlicality for each SH . Let Hℓ (SH ) denote the Helicality\n552\t\n553\t\fall ℓ\n554\t(d)\n555\t\n556\t(d)\n557\t\n558\typeak (SH ) = max Hℓ (SH ).\n559\tall ℓ\n560\t\n561\t(7)\n562\t\n563\tWe refer to ysum as the layer-sum Helicality and ypeak as\n564\tthe layer-max Helicality. “all ℓ” means these calculations\n565\ttake into account all the Transformer layers. ysum represents the overall strength of the internal pitch helix across\n566\tall layers, while ypeak represents the strength of the most\n567\tprominent layer.\n568\t\n569\t20\n570\t\n571\tFrequency\n572\t\n573\tat layer ℓ. We then define the dependent variables as:\n574\tX\n575\t(d)\n576\t(d)\n577\tysum (SH ) =\n578\tHℓ (SH ),\n579\t(6)\n580\t\n581\tMean: 2.289\n582\tMedian: 1.982\n583\tRandom: 0.371\n584\t\n585\t10\n586\t00\n587\t\n588\t2\n589\t\n590\t4\n591\t6\n592\t8\n593\tLayer-max Helicality\n594\t\n595\tFigure 7: Distribution of layer-max Helicality across all\n596\tinstruments for Jukebox. The dashed lines indicate the\n597\tmean, median, and the reference score.\n598\t\n599\t4.1.2 Data\n600\t3.4.3 Multiple Regression Analysis and Statistical Testing\n601\tWe represent each harmonic condition (H, d) using binary\n602\tvariables corresponding to combinations of harmonic indices and spectral tilt conditions, and analyze their relationship with Helicality via multiple regression. The binary variable is defined as:\n603\t(\n604\t1, if (h ∈ H ∧ d′ = d)\n605\t(8)\n606\txh,d =\n607\t0, otherwise,\n608\twhere d′ denotes the spectral tilt of the given condition.\n609\tFor each dependent variable y ∈ {ysum , ypeak }, we assume the following linear model:\n610\tX\n611\ty = β0 +\n612\tβh,d xh,d + ε,\n613\t(9)\n614\t(h,d)∈(Hall \\{1})×D\n615\t\n616\twhere β0 is the intercept, βh,d is the regression coefficient\n617\trepresenting the contribution of harmonic h under the tilt\n618\tcondition d, × denotes the Cartesian product, and ε is the\n619\terror term. The fundamental component h = 1 is excluded from the regression, so the summation is taken over\n620\tHall \\ {1}. We construct (xh,d , y) for all harmonic conditions and estimate the coefficients βh,d using ordinary least\n621\tsquares. We then perform an F-test to evaluate the significance of the overall regression model, followed by t-tests\n622\tfor each coefficient to assess whether the corresponding\n623\t(h, d) has a statistically significant effect on Helicality.\n624\t4. EXPERIMENTS\n625\t4.1 Experimental Setup\n626\t4.1.1 Models\n627\tWe analyze two trained MFMs: Jukebox and MusicGen.\n628\tJukebox. We used the top-level decoder of the 5B model\n629\tfrom jukemirlib [6] 1 . The Transformer decoder consists of 72 layers, and the dimensionality of the intermediate representations is 4, 800.\n630\tMusicGen. We used the large model (3.3B) 2 . Text conditioning was set to an empty string, and the input music signals were encoded using the frozen EnCodec. MusicGen’s\n631\tTransformer decoder has 48 layers, and the dimensionality\n632\tof the intermediate representations is 2, 048.\n633\t1 Available by appending a file name (e.g., 5b/vqvae.pth.tar,\n634\t5b/prior_level_2.pth.tar) to https://openaipublic.\n635\tazureedge.net/jukebox/models/.\n636\t2 https://huggingface.co/facebook/\n637\tmusicgen-large\n638\t\n639\tWe used two types of datasets: (i) instrument note dataset\n640\tand (ii) artificial test signal dataset. For both datasets, 12\n641\tpitch classes spanning 3 octaves (C3–B5, Nkey = 36) were\n642\trendered as half notes at 120 BPM.\n643\tInstrument note dataset. We used the notes subset of the SynTheory [7] dataset, which contains isolated musical notes from 92 instruments synthesized with\n644\tTimGM6mb.sf2 [25], based on General MIDI [26] program numbers and instrument categories.\n645\tArtificial test signal dataset. We synthesized artificial signals based on Eq. (3), in which harmonic components were explicitly controlled. We defined Hall as\n646\t{ 41 , 13 , 12 , 1, . . . , 8}. Hs were set to cover possible choices\n647\tfrom Hall , and spectral tilt conditions D = {flat, decay}\n648\twere applied to each H, resulting in 137 harmonic conditions in total.\n649\tPreprocessing. Original signals were 4-second stereo audio sampled at 44.1 kHz, and they were converted to\n650\tmonaural. For MusicGen, 4-second, 32 kHz, monaural\n651\tformat was used. For Jukebox, the original signals were\n652\trepeated or trimmed to be 24 seconds.\n653\t4.1.3 Parameter Optimization\n654\tWe optimized the parameters of Eq. (1) using Optuna [27].\n655\tEach optimization used more than 1, 000 trials and was\n656\trepeated three times with different random seeds. These\n657\thyperparameters were chosen based on preliminary experiments to ensure that the MSE values sufficiently converged. The same search ranges were used for all layers,\n658\tinstruments, and models, e.g., the search range for ωchroma\n659\twas set to [− π2 , − π6 ] ∪ [ π6 , π2 ].\n660\t4.2 Comparison among Instruments\n661\tWe investigated whether MFMs embed pitch in a helical\n662\tstructure, and if so, how the degree of Helicality varies\n663\tacross different instruments. For each instrument, we computed Helicality at each layer from its signals, and defined\n664\tthe layer-max Helicality as the score for that instrument.\n665\tIn addition, we computed Helicality for three-dimensional\n666\tuniform random variables (“Random”) as a reference. We\n667\tgenerated random variables 100 times and computed their\n668\tHelicality, obtaining a score of 0.371 ± 0.014. If the Helicality of a given instrument exceeds this value, we regard\n669\tit as exhibiting a helical structure.\n670\tFigure 7 shows a histogram of Helicality, and Fig. 8\n671\t\n672\t\f3.0\n673\t\n674\t±1 std\n675\t\n676\tMean Helicality\n677\t\n678\t2.5\n679\t2.0\n680\t1.5\n681\t1.0\n682\t\n683\t3.592\n684\t3.664\n685\t3.697\n686\t3.937\n687\t4.081\n688\t4.612\n689\t5.455\n690\t5.663\n691\t6.054\n692\t\n693\t0.5\n694\t0\n695\t\n696\t4\n697\t6\n698\t8\n699\tLayer-max Helicality\n700\t\n701\t9.410\n702\t\n703\tFigure 8: Best / worst 10 instruments by layer-max Helicality for Jukebox.\n704\tElectric clean guitar\n705\t\n706\t10\n707\t\n708\t20\n709\t\n710\t30\n711\t\n712\t40\n713\t\n714\tLayer\n715\t\n716\t50\n717\t\n718\t60\n719\t\n720\t70\n721\t\n722\tFigure 10: Mean Helicality across instruments at each\n723\tlayer of Jukebox.\n724\t\n725\tOr\n726\tga\n727\tBa n tim Layer-max Helicality\n728\tG ss bre\n729\tCh uitar timbr s\n730\trom Pia tim es\n731\tati no t bre\n732\tc p im s\n733\te b\n734\tB\n735\tEn ras rcus res\n736\tsem s sio\n737\tble timb n\n738\ttim res\n739\tS\n740\ty\n741\tStr nt bres\n742\ti h\n743\tEth ng ti lea\n744\tnic mb d\n745\tt re\n746\tSy imbr s\n747\tRe nth es\n748\ted\n749\tPip tim pad\n750\te t bre\n751\tim s\n752\tbre\n753\ts\n754\t\n755\tInstrument\n756\t\n757\tOcarina 0.812\n758\tKalimba 0.887\n759\tRecorder 0.897\n760\tPiccolo 0.917\n761\tAlto sax 0.948\n762\tXylophone 0.972\n763\t1.003\n764\tSitar\n765\t1.032\n766\tMarimba\n767\t1.055\n768\tEnglish horn\n769\tSynth .voice . 1.055\n770\t..\n771\t..\n772\tOverdriven guitar\n773\tChurch organ\n774\tFretless bass\n775\tDulcimer\n776\tMusic box\n777\tSlap bass 2\n778\tHarpsichord\n779\tPlucked electric bass\n780\tElectric clean guitar\n781\tHammond organ\n782\t0\n783\t2\n784\t\n785\t7.5\n786\t5.0\n787\t\n788\tHammond organ\n789\t\n790\t2.5\n791\t\n792\tPC1\n793\t\n794\tPC4\n795\t\n796\tPC3\n797\t\n798\tPC3\n799\t\n800\tPC1\n801\t\n802\tPC2\n803\t\n804\tFigure 9: Jukebox embeddings for two instruments at the\n805\tlayer and three-PC projection corresponding to each instrument’s layer-max Helicality.\n806\tshows the best and worst 10 instruments ranked by Helicality. The model used to obtain these results is Jukebox.\n807\tMFMs embed pitch in a helical structure. As shown\n808\tin Fig. 7, the Helicality of all instruments exceeds that of\n809\t“Random”. Although not shown due to space limitations,\n810\tsimilar results are observed for MusicGen. Therefore, we\n811\tconclude that both analyzed MFMs embed pitch in a helical structure. However, the average Helicality across all\n812\tinstruments differs between models (Jukebox: 2.28, MusicGen: 1.16).\n813\tThe helical structure varies depending on instrument timbre. We focus on the differences across instruments. As shown in Fig. 8, the scores vary widely, with a\n814\tminimum of 0.812 for ocarina and a maximum of 9.410 for\n815\thammond organ. As shown in Fig. 7, most instruments are\n816\tdistributed in a moderate range from 1 to 3, while a subset\n817\texhibits extremely high scores. Figure 9 shows examples\n818\tof helical structures for different instruments. These examples illustrate that the helical structures differ across instruments, indicating that instrument timbre influences the\n819\tgeometry of the internal pitch helix. For instance, while\n820\tthe hammond organ exhibits a clear helix with one rotation\n821\tper octave, the electric clean guitar shows a helix with two\n822\trotations per octave. A similar trend is observed for MusicGen. Comparing the instrument rankings as illustrated\n823\tin Fig. 8 between Jukebox and MusicGen, we obtained a\n824\tlinear correlation coefficient of r = 0.59. These results\n825\tsuggest that the relative ordering of Helicality across instruments tends to be consistent across different models.\n826\tThe prominent layer tends to appear in deeper lay-\n827\t\n828\tInstrument category\n829\t\n830\tFigure 11: Distribution of layer-max Helicality across instrument categories for Jukebox. Each point represents the\n831\tscore of an individual instrument.\n832\t\n833\ters. Figure 10 shows the mean Helicality across instruments at each layer. The mean Helicality peaks in deeper\n834\tlayers. These results suggest that abstract concepts such as\n835\tpitch are more clearly represented in deeper layers. This\n836\ttendency is consistent with prior probing studies [7, 8],\n837\twhere deeper layers tend to perform better on downstream\n838\ttasks. This suggests that Helicality may be related to\n839\tdownstream performance.\n840\tHelicality shows trends across instrument categories. Figure 11 shows the distribution of Helicality\n841\tacross instrument categories based on General MIDI. Organ timbres show the highest mean with large variance.\n842\tBass, guitar, and piano timbres exhibit moderate means\n843\twith similarly high variability. In contrast, brass and string\n844\ttimbres show relatively stable distributions. These results\n845\tsuggest that Helicality is influenced by both global tendencies corresponding to timbral characteristics of instrument\n846\tcategories and instrument-specific acoustic factors.\n847\t4.3 Analysis Using Artificial Test Signals\n848\tIn Sec. 4.2, we investigated whether instrument timbre\n849\tcontributes to the formation of the internal pitch helix. As\n850\tdescribed in Section 2.3, instrument timbre can be characterized by the presence and amplitude of harmonic components. Based on the multiple regression analysis described in Sec. 3.4, we analyzed whether the presence of\n851\teach harmonic component and the spectral tilt contribute\n852\tto the clarity of the internal pitch helix. The dependent\n853\t\n854\t\f*\n855\t\n856\t**\n857\t\n858\tDecay\n859\tFlat\n860\t\n861\t**\n862\t\n863\t0\n864\t1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n865\t\n866\tCoefficient\n867\t\n868\t*\n869\t\n870\t0.25\n871\t\n872\t*\n873\t\n874\t*\n875\t\n876\t3\n877\t\n878\t4\n879\t\n880\t**\n881\t\n882\t5\n883\t\n884\t6\n885\t\n886\t7\n887\t\n888\t8\n889\t\n890\tCoefficient\n891\t\n892\t1\n893\t\n894\t20\n895\t\n896\t*\n897\t\n898\tJukebox: ysum\n899\t\n900\t*\n901\t\n902\t20\n903\t\n904\t1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n905\t\n906\tMusicGen: ypeak\n907\t\n908\t*\n909\t\n910\tDecay\n911\tFlat\n912\t\n913\t*\n914\t\n915\t0.00\n916\t\n917\t**\n918\t\n919\t5\n920\t\n921\t**\n922\t\n923\t*\n924\t\n925\t3\n926\t\n927\t4\n928\t\n929\tHarmonic\n930\t\n931\t*6* 7\n932\t\n933\t*\n934\t\n935\t5\n936\t\n937\t8\n938\t\n939\t*\n940\t\n941\t3\n942\t\n943\t4\n944\t\n945\t5\n946\t\n947\t*6* 7\n948\t\n949\t8\n950\t\n951\tMusicGen: ysum\n952\t\n953\t*\n954\t\n955\t0\n956\t\n957\t1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n958\t\n959\tDecay\n960\tFlat\n961\t\n962\t**\n963\t\n964\t0\n965\t\n966\tCoefficient\n967\t\n968\tCoefficient\n969\t\n970\tJukebox: ypeak\n971\t\n972\t**\n973\t\n974\t1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n975\t\n976\tDecay\n977\tFlat\n978\t*\n979\t\n980\t3\n981\t\n982\t4\n983\t\n984\tHarmonic\n985\t\n986\t**\n987\t5\n988\t\n989\t* *\n990\t*6 7 8\n991\t\n992\tFigure 12: Regression coefficients for Jukebox and MusicGen. Asterisks indicate statistical significance (p < 0.05).\n993\t\n994\tHarmonic set H\n995\t\n996\tSpectral tilt\n997\t\n998\t1\n999\t2\n1000\t3\n1001\t4\n1002\t5\n1003\t\n1004\t{ 12 , 1, 2, 4, 8}\n1005\t{ 14 , 12 , 1, 2, 4, 8}\n1006\t{ 12 , 1, 2}\n1007\t{ 12 , 1, 2}\n1008\t{ 14 , 12 , 1, 2, 4, 8}\n1009\t\n1010\t.\n1011\t.\n1012\t.\n1013\t133\n1014\t134\n1015\t135\n1016\t136\n1017\t137\n1018\t\n1019\tRank\n1020\t\n1021\tPeak layer\n1022\t\n1023\typeak\n1024\t\n1025\tdecay\n1026\tdecay\n1027\tflat\n1028\tdecay\n1029\tflat\n1030\t\n1031\t52\n1032\t51\n1033\t50\n1034\t56\n1035\t53\n1036\t\n1037\t7.744\n1038\t7.688\n1039\t6.906\n1040\t6.469\n1041\t5.097\n1042\t\n1043\t.\n1044\t.\n1045\t.\n1046\t\n1047\t.\n1048\t.\n1049\t.\n1050\t\n1051\t.\n1052\t.\n1053\t.\n1054\t\n1055\t.\n1056\t.\n1057\t.\n1058\t\n1059\t{1} (pure tone)\n1060\t{1, 6, 8}\n1061\t{1, 6, 8}\n1062\t{1, 7}\n1063\t{1, 7, 8}\n1064\t\n1065\t–\n1066\tflat\n1067\tdecay\n1068\tdecay\n1069\tdecay\n1070\t\n1071\t21\n1072\t36\n1073\t1\n1074\t0\n1075\t0\n1076\t\n1077\t0.619\n1078\t0.594\n1079\t0.593\n1080\t0.589\n1081\t0.583\n1082\t\n1083\tTable 1: Best and worst 5 examples of layer-max Helicality for artificial test signals in Jukebox.\n1084\t\n1085\tvariables were defined as ysum and ypeak .\n1086\tFigure 12 shows the regression coefficients for ysum and\n1087\typeak in both Jukebox and MusicGen. Table 1 shows examples of layer-max Helicality obtained for each harmonic\n1088\tcondition in Jukebox. The F-tests indicate that the regression models are statistically significant for both ysum and\n1089\typeak in both models. Therefore, we interpret the contributions of each (h, d) condition based on the corresponding\n1090\tt-tests of the regression coefficients.\n1091\tOctave-equivalent harmonics form the helix. As\n1092\tshown in Fig. 12, the {2, 4, 8}-th harmonics and the\n1093\t{1/2, 1/4} subharmonic components tend to exhibit positive coefficients, with p-values below 0.05 in most cases.\n1094\tConsistently, as seen in Tab. 1, artificial signals with high\n1095\tHelicality are composed exclusively of harmonics whose\n1096\tfrequency ratios are powers of 2. A similar trend is observed for MusicGen, with a linear correlation of r = 0.78\n1097\tbetween the models based on the Helicality rankings of\n1098\tartificial signals. These findings align with octave equivalence in human pitch perception and suggest that harmonic\n1099\tcomponents with frequency ratios that are powers of 2 play\n1100\tan important role in forming the internal pitch helix within\n1101\tMFMs. Furthermore, this interpretation is supported by\n1102\tSec. 4.2: the hammond organ, which achieves the highest Helicality, exhibits a spectrum dominated by octaveequivalent harmonics, as shown in Fig. 3.\n1103\t\n1104\tNon-octave-equivalent harmonics hinder helix formation. In contrast, the remaining {3, 5, 6, 7}-th harmonics and the 1/3 subharmonic component generally show\n1105\tnegative coefficients. These results suggest that harmonics that are not octave-equivalent inhibit the formation of\n1106\tthe helix. Indeed, the recorder, which has a low Helicality,\n1107\texhibits a spectrum dominated by non-octave-equivalent\n1108\tharmonics, as shown in Fig. 3.\n1109\tSpectral decay facilitates helix formation. Focusing\n1110\ton the spectral tilt conditions, the decay condition tends to\n1111\tshow larger coefficients than the flat condition for octaveequivalent harmonics. Compared to the flat condition with\n1112\tequal harmonic amplitudes, the decay condition, which resembles natural spectral decay, yields stronger contributions. This suggests that MFMs may form an internal pitch\n1113\thelix more effectively from spectral distributions that resemble those of natural instrument sounds.\n1114\tSignals without harmonics do not form a helix. As\n1115\tshown in Tab. 1, pure tone exhibits low Helicality. Moreover, the ocarina, which has the lowest Helicality, behaves\n1116\tas a single-mode Helmholtz resonator [20] and lacks a\n1117\tclear harmonic series. Therefore, it can be regarded as\n1118\ta condition similar to pure tone, leading to low Helicality. These observations indicate that signals without harmonic structure are less likely to form an internal pitch\n1119\thelix, which is consistent with the findings regarding the\n1120\tpresence of harmonics described above.\n1121\t5. CONCLUSION\n1122\tThis study demonstrated that the intermediate representations of two generative MFMs embed pitch information in\n1123\ta helical structure. The clarity and geometry of this structure vary not only across the two analyzed models but also\n1124\twith the acoustic properties of the input, particularly its\n1125\tharmonic composition. These results suggest that MFMs\n1126\trepresent pitch not merely as frequency differences, but\n1127\tas a geometric structure grounded in harmonic relationships. However, whether the helical structure originates\n1128\tin the Transformer or earlier representations remains unclear. Further work should also examine its generality\n1129\tacross models and training data, as well as its relationship\n1130\tto downstream task performance.\n1131\t\n1132\t\f6. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\n1133\tThis work was supported by JST FOREST Grant Number JPMJFR226V and JSPS KAKENHI Grant Number\n1134\tJP26K21256.\n1135\t7. REFERENCES\n1136\t[1] P. Dhariwal, H. Jun, C. Payne, J. W. Kim, A. Radford,\n1137\tand I. Sutskever, “Jukebox: A generative model for\n1138\tmusic,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.00341, 2020.\n1139\t[2] J. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez, “Simple and controllable music generation,” in NeurIPS, 2023.\n1140\t[3] A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel,\n1141\tM. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen,\n1142\tA. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank, “MusicLM: Generating music\n1143\tfrom text,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325, 2023.\n1144\t[4] Y. Li, R. Yuan, G. Zhang, Y. Ma, X. Chen,\n1145\tH. Yin, C. Xiao, C. Lin, A. Ragni, E. Benetos,\n1146\tN. Gyenge, R. Dannenberg, R. Liu, W. Chen, G. Xia,\n1147\tY. Shi, W. Huang, Z. Wang, Y. Guo, and J. Fu,\n1148\t“MERT: Acoustic music understanding model with\n1149\tlarge-scale self-supervised training,” arXiv preprint\n1150\tarXiv:2306.00107, 2023.\n1151\t[5] W.-H. Liao, Y. Takida, Y. Ikemiya, Z. Zhong, C.H. Lai, G. Fabbro, K. Shimada, K. Toyama, K. W.\n1152\tCheuk, M. A. Martínez-Ramírez, S. Takahashi, S. Uhlich, T. Akama, W. Choi, Y. Koyama, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n1153\t“Music foundation model as generic booster for music\n1154\tdownstream tasks,” TMLR, 2025.\n1155\t[6] R. Castellon, C. Donahue, and P. Liang, “Codified audio language modeling learns useful representations\n1156\tfor music information retrieval,” in ISMIR, 2021.\n1157\t\n1158\t[12] Z. Liu, O. Kitouni, N. Nolte, E. J. Michaud,\n1159\tM. Tegmark, and M. Williams, “Towards understanding grokking: An effective theory of representation\n1160\tlearning,” in NeurIPS, 2022.\n1161\t[13] B. Heinzerling and K. Inui, “Monotonic representation of numeric attributes in language models,” in ACL,\n1162\t2024, pp. 175–195.\n1163\t[14] R. Marjieh, V. Veselovsky, T. L. Griffiths, and\n1164\tI. Sucholutsky, “What is a number, that a large\n1165\tlanguage model may know it?”\n1166\tarXiv preprint\n1167\tarXiv:2502.01540, 2025.\n1168\t[15] F. Zhu, D. Dai, and Z. Sui, “Language models encode\n1169\tthe value of numbers linearly,” in COLING, 2025, pp.\n1170\t693–709.\n1171\t[16] G. Alain and Y. Bengio, “Understanding intermediate\n1172\tlayers using linear classifier probes,” in ICLR, 2017.\n1173\t[17] C. Papaioannou, E. Benetos, and A. Potamianos, “Universal music representations? evaluating foundation\n1174\tmodels on world music corpora,” in ISMIR, 2025.\n1175\t[18] V. Lostanlen, S. Sridhar, B. McFee, A. Farnsworth, and\n1176\tJ. P. Bello, “Learning the helix topology of musical\n1177\tpitch,” in IEEE ICASSP, 2020, pp. 11–15.\n1178\t[19] S. Sridhar and V. Lostanlen, “Helicality: An isomapbased measure of octave equivalence in audio data,” in\n1179\tISMIR, 2020.\n1180\t[20] N. H. Fletcher and T. D. Rossing, The Physics of Musical Instruments, 2nd ed. Springer-Verlag, 1998.\n1181\t[21] A. van den Oord, O. Vinyals, and K. Kavukcuoglu,\n1182\t“Neural discrete representation learning,” in NeurIPS,\n1183\tvol. 30, 2017.\n1184\t[22] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit,\n1185\tL. Jones, A. N. Gomez, L. u. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin,\n1186\t“Attention is all you need,” in NeurIPS, vol. 30, 2017.\n1187\t\n1188\t[7] M. Wei, M. Freeman, C. Donahue, and C. Sun, “Do\n1189\tmusic generation models encode music theory?” in ISMIR, 2024.\n1190\t\n1191\t[23] A. Défossez, J. Copet, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “High\n1192\tFidelity Neural Audio Compression,” in ICLR, 2024.\n1193\t\n1194\t[8] W. Ma, X. Li, and G. Xia, “Do music LLMs learn symbolic concepts? a pilot study using probing and intervention,” in NeurIPS Workshop on Audio Imagination,\n1195\t2024.\n1196\t\n1197\t[24] C. Raffel, N. Shazeer, A. Roberts, K. Lee, S. Narang,\n1198\tM. Matena, Y. Zhou, W. Li, and P. J. Liu, “Exploring the limits of transfer learning with a unified textto-text transformer,” Journal of machine learning research, vol. 21, no. 140, pp. 1–67, 2020.\n1199\t\n1200\t[9] R. N. Shepard, “Geometrical approximations to the\n1201\tstructure of musical pitch,” Psychological Review,\n1202\tvol. 89, no. 4, pp. 305–333, 1982.\n1203\t\n1204\t[25] T. Brechbill, “Timidity++,” https://timbrechbill.com/\n1205\tsaxguru/Timidity.php, 2004.\n1206\t\n1207\t[10] A. Radford, J. Wu, R. Child, D. Luan, D. Amodei, and\n1208\tI. Sutskever, “Language models are unsupervised multitask learners,” OpenAI blog, vol. 1, no. 8, p. 9, 2019.\n1209\t[11] J. Engels, E. J. Michaud, I. Liao, W. Gurnee, and\n1210\tM. Tegmark, “Not all language model features are onedimensionally linear,” in ICLR, 2025.\n1211\t\n1212\t[26] “General midi system level 1,” the MIDI Manufacturers Association, Tech. Rep., 1991.\n1213\t[27] T. Akiba, S. Sano, T. Yanase, T. Ohta, and M. Koyama,\n1214\t“Optuna: A next-generation hyperparameter optimization framework,” in ACM SIGKDD, 2019, pp. 2623–\n1215\t2631.\n1216\t\n1217\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "b6f8e0ef-c018-40d1-a8ae-7e1e4174473e", "uuid": "94279050-fe8e-49d3-8eaa-fd4e5bf24cf9", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T05:52:49.248Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.29086.txt", "content": "DO MUSIC FOUNDATION MODELS\nEMBED PITCH IN HELICAL STRUCTURE?\nHayato Yagi∗,1\nShinnosuke Takamichi∗,1,2\nRin Sato3\nKeitaro Tanaka3\nShigeo Morishima3\n1\n2\n3\nKeio University, Japan\nThe University of Tokyo, Japan\nWaseda University, Japan\n∗\n\n{hayatobuti523,shinnosuke_takamichi}@keio.jp\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\nFoundation models have recently advanced music information processing, following their success in natural language\nprocessing (NLP). In particular, self-supervised learning\n(SSL) on large-scale music data has produced powerful\nmusic-specific models, known as music foundation models (MFMs) [1–5]. MFMs exhibit strong versatility in both\nmusic generation and understanding tasks such as classification and analysis, drawing attention for their potential to\nsupport new forms of music creation [5, 6].\nDespite their impressive capabilities, the internal mechanisms of MFMs remain unclear. In particular, how they\nacquire and use musical knowledge such as pitch and harmony is not well understood. To address this, recent studies have analyzed intermediate representations learned by\nMFMs, using probing methods to implicitly evaluate what\nmusical knowledge is encoded [7, 8]. Trained on humancomposed music, MFMs may encode such knowledge as\nlatent structures. However, it still remains unclear how\nsuch knowledge is explicitly represented as a geometric\nstructure. Clarifying this would provide important insights into whether MFMs understand musical knowledge\nas structured representations.\nAs a first step toward understanding the internal geometry of MFMs, we focus on pitch, a fundamental musi© H. Yagi, S. Takamichi, R. Sato, K. Tanaka, and S. Morishima. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\nLicense (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: H. Yagi, S. Takamichi, R. Sato, K.\nTanaka, and S. Morishima, “Do Music Foundation Models Embed Pitch\nin Helical Structure?”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n\nInstruments\n\nFoundation model\n\nSingle note\n\n× 𝑁key\n\nArtificial test signal\nflat\n𝑓0 2𝑓0 3𝑓0 4𝑓0\n\n2. Extraction\n\ndecay\n𝑓0 2𝑓0 3𝑓0 4𝑓0\n\n…\n\n3. Evaluation\n3D features\n\nTransformer\nTransformer\nTransformer\n\nThis study analyzes the intermediate representations of\nmusic foundation models (MFMs) and reports the geometric structures used to represent pitch information. By inputting isolated musical notes into trained MFMs and analyzing their principal components, we reveal that the representations form a helical structure reflecting the octave\nperiodicity of pitch. Furthermore, we show that the clarity\nand geometry of this helical structure vary not only across\nmodels but also with the acoustic properties of the input\nsignals. Our analysis provides a novel approach for clarifying the internal mechanisms of MFMs.\n\n1. Input: Various timbres\n\nTransformer\nTransformer\n\narXiv:2607.29086v1 [cs.SD] 31 Jul 2026\n\nABSTRACT\n\nPCA\nFitting\n\nExtract\nIntermediate\nrepresentation\n\nHelicality\n\nFigure 1: Overview. We extract pitch-dependent features\nfrom a music foundation model and evaluate their helical\nstructure using PCA and parametric fitting.\n\ncal element. In music psychology, pitch has long been\nmodeled as a helical structure combining pitch height and\noctave periodicity [9]. We hypothesize that MFMs may\nexhibit this structure and investigate whether their intermediate representations form a pitch helix. We extract\npitch-related features from the representations and quantify the degree to which they follow a helical structure using a dedicated metric (Fig. 1). In this study, we analyze\ntwo generative MFMs and show that (1) the helical structure emerges across a wide range of instrument sounds,\nand (2) this structure is primarily driven by specific harmonics, as revealed by experiments with artificial test signals. Our code is available at https://github.com/\ntakamichi-lab/mfm-pitch-helix.\n2. RELATED WORK\n2.1 Interpretability of Foundation Models\nUnderstanding the intermediate representations of foundation models has become a key research topic, especially in\nNLP. Prior work shows that models like GPT-2 [10] encode\nperiodic concepts (e.g., days, months) as circular geometric patterns [11], suggesting they form human-like conceptual structures beyond simple statistical patterns [12–15].\nIn music information processing, probing [16] has been\nwidely used to assess whether intermediate representations\nencode musical attributes by measuring how well a linear classifier predicts them. It has been applied to MFMs\nsuch as Jukebox [1] and MERT [4], demonstrating that in-\n\n\fheight\n\nAcoustic grand piano\n\nViolin\n\nRecorder\n\nSawtooth wave lead\n\nHammond organ\n\nAcoustic steel guitar\n\nC4\nC3\nG F# F\nE\nAG#\nA# B class DD#\nC C#\nFigure 2: Shepard’s pitch helix [9]. Vertical height and\ncircular angle correspond to pitch height and pitch class,\nrespectively.\n\ntermediate layers encode musical attributes such as pitch,\nchords, tempo, and genres [4, 5, 7, 8, 17]. Some of the studies [7, 8] also report that deeper layers better encode pitch\nand chords. However, these studies primarily assess what\nmusical attributes are encoded in the intermediate representations via probe performance. Thus, unlike in NLP,\nresearch that directly examines how music-theoretical concepts are represented as geometric structures in MFMs remains limited.\n2.2 Pitch Helix\nIn music psychology, pitch is conceptualized along two\nperceptual dimensions: pitch height, corresponding to absolute frequency, and pitch class, representing note identity\nwithin the twelve-tone octave cycle (e.g., C, C♯, D), regardless of octave. This dual representation leads to the principle of octave equivalence, where tones that are one octave\napart are perceived as similar. To capture this property,\nthe pitch helix has been proposed as a model that arranges\npitches in a three-dimensional spiral [9]. In this structure,\nvertical position reflects pitch height, while the angular position represents pitch class, resulting in tones recurring at\nthe same angular position in every octave (Fig. 2).\nRecent work shows that such helical structures can\nemerge from raw music signals without supervision [18].\nHowever, it lacks quantitative evaluation. To overcome\nthis, a metric called Helicality has been proposed to measure how closely three-dimensional points fit to an ideal\nhelix [19]. In our study, we apply the Helicality to the intermediate representations of MFMs in order to determine\nwhether they encode pitch in a helical form.\n2.3 Timbre and Harmonic Structure\nInstrument sounds are complex tones composed of a fundamental frequency f0 and multiple harmonics. The frequencies, amplitudes, and temporal evolution of these\ncomponents determine timbre. The harmonic structure depends on the sound production mechanism and physical\nproperties of the vibrating body, and varies across instrument types such as string, wind, and percussion [20].\nAs shown in Fig. 3, even for the same pitch (C4), amplitude distributions around f0 and harmonic components\nvary across instruments. Such differences likely influence\nthe internal pitch representations learned by MFMs.\n\nFigure 3: Amplitude spectra for various instruments. The\nred dashed line indicates the fundamental frequency f0 ,\nand the black dashed lines indicate subharmonics f0 /h or\nharmonics hf0 , where h is an integer.\n\n2.4 Music Foundation Models\nWe focus on two generative MFMs used in our experiments: Jukebox [1] and MusicGen [2], both pretrained on\nlarge-scale music data via SSL.\nJukebox. Jukebox uses a vector quantized variational autoencoder [21] to convert music signals into discrete tokens, which are then autoregressively predicted by a hierarchical Transformer [22] decoder. The decoder consists\nof three levels: the top captures long-term musical features,\nwhile the middle and bottom refine temporal resolution.\nMusicGen. MusicGen is a non-hierarchical generative\nmodel consisting of a pretrained neural audio codec [23],\na T5 text encoder [24], and a Transformer decoder.\n\n3. METHOD\n3.1 Extracting Pitch-Dependent Features\nAs shown in Fig. 1, we input monophonic music signals with specific pitches into the model’s pretrained audio tokenizer and pass the resulting token sequences to the\nTransformer decoder. Following prior work [7], we obtain a time series of intermediate representations from each\nTransformer-decoder layer and average them over time to\nobtain one vector per isolated musical note. Repeating this\nprocess for Nkey pitches yields a set of pitch-conditioned\nintermediate representation vectors for each layer.\nTo capture pitch-related structure, we apply principal\ncomponent analysis (PCA) to the set of pitch-conditioned\nintermediate representations obtained from each layer. We\ncompute \u0001the top five principal components and enumerate all 53 = 10 combinations of three components. For\neach combination, the pitch-conditioned representations\nare projected into a three-dimensional space, resulting\nin ten different sets of three-dimensional embeddings for\neach layer. These embeddings are then used to evaluate\nthe degree to which each layer encodes a helical structure\nwith respect to pitch.\n\n\fchroma\nchroma\n\n00\n\n𝜃(𝑡)\n==\n𝜔chroma\n(𝑡 −\n𝑡0 )𝑡0 )\n𝜃(𝑡)\n𝜔chroma\n(𝑡 −\n\n𝜃(𝑡)\n= =\n𝜔chroma\nℎ(𝑡)\nℎpitch (𝑡\n∙ 𝑡−\n+ 𝑡ℎ00)\n𝑟(𝑡) = 𝑟slope ∙ 𝑡 + 𝑟0\n𝜃(𝑡) = 𝜔chroma (𝑡 − 𝑡0 )\n𝒙𝑡\n𝒚(𝑡)\n𝒙\n𝒚(𝑡)𝒚(𝑡)\nℎ𝑡pitch\n𝒙𝒙𝑡𝑡 = 0.1 𝒚(𝑡)\n𝒙\n𝒚(𝑡)\n𝒙\n𝒚(𝑡)\n𝑡\n𝑡\nℎ\n= 0.2\n\nℎpitch = 0.1\n==\n0.1\nℎℎpitch\n(a)ℎpitch\nℎpitch\n=0.2\n0.1\n0.1\npitch =\n\nZ\n\n0.1\nℎℎpitch\n===\n0.2\n=\n0.1\npitch\n𝑟slope\npitch\nℎpitch\n=0.1\n0.2\nℎℎ\n0.2\npitch =\n0.2\n𝑟ℎ\n===\n0.1\nℎ\n=\n0.2\npitch\n𝑟slope\n0.0\nslope\npitch\n𝑟\n=\n0.1\n𝑟\n= 0.1\nslope\nslope\n\n0.0\n𝑟𝑟slope\n==−0.1\n=\n0.0\nslope\nslope\n𝜔𝑟𝑟𝑟chroma\n𝜋/3\n==−0.1\n−0.1\nslope =\nslope\n=\n−0.1\n𝑟\n−0.1\n𝜔𝑟slope\n=\n𝜋/3\nslope ==\n𝜔𝜔\n𝜋/6\nchroma\nchroma\n𝜔\n=\n𝜋/3\n=\n𝜋/3\nchroma\nchroma\n𝜋/3\n(c)𝜔𝜔chroma\n==𝜋/6\n𝜔\n=\n𝜋/3\nchroma\n𝜔chroma\n= 𝜋/6\n𝜋/6\n𝜔\n=\nchroma\nchroma\n\n𝜔chroma\n= 𝜋/6\n𝜔chroma\n= 𝜋/6\n\n𝒙𝑡\n\nZ\n\n𝒚(𝑡)\n\nℋ =\n\n𝒚(𝑡)\n\nℎpitch\n𝑟 = 0.1\n= 0.1\n\n1 1\n, , 1, 2, 4, 8\n4 2\n\nℋ = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\n\nslope\n\nℎpitch\n𝑟 = 0.2\n= 0.0\n\n𝒙𝑡\n\nslope\n\nslope\nslope\n\n0.1\n==0.0\n=\n0.1\nslope\nslope\n(b)𝑟𝑟\n𝑟slope\n=−0.1\n0.0\n𝑟𝑟𝑟slope==\n0.0\n\npitch\n\n𝒙𝑡\n\nZ\n\n𝑟slope\n= 0.1\nℎ\n= −0.1\n0.1\n𝑟 pitch\n=\nslope\n\n𝒚(𝑡)\n\nZ\n\n𝑟slope\n= 0.0\nℎpitch\n= 0.2\n𝜔\nchroma = 𝜋/3\n𝑟slope\n= −0.1\n𝑟slope\n= 0.1\n𝜔\nchroma = 𝜋/6\nZ\n\n𝜔chroma\n𝑟slope = 𝜋/3\n0.0\n𝜔chroma\n𝑟slope = 𝜋/6\n−0.1\nX\n\nZ\n\nX\n\n𝜔chroma in\n= 𝜋/3\nFigure 4: Effects of parameters\nEq. (1). (a) hpitch\n𝜔\n= 𝜋/6\ncontrols vertical spacing, (b) rchroma\nslope affects conicity (positive: expanding, negative: contracting), (c) ωchroma sets\nthe number of pitches per rotation. Each column shows a\n3D view (left) and its xz-plane projection (right).\n\n3.2 Parametric Pitch Helix Model\nWe model the three-dimensional embeddings using a parametric helical function defined by nine parameters: the\nheight offset h0 , height slope hpitch , radius offset r0 , radius\nslope rslope , angular frequency ωchroma , phase offset p0 ,\nand three orthonormal basis vectors c, u, v ∈ R3 . Here, c\nrepresents the central axis of the helix, while u and v span\nthe rotation plane. These parameters control the position,\nshape, and pitch-wise rotation of the helix.\nUsing these parameters, the helix function y(p) for the\nsemitone-ordered pitch index p = 1, . . . , Nkey is given by:\ny(p) = h(p) · c + r(p) (cos θ(p) · u + sin θ(p) · v)\n\n\nh(p) = hpitch · p + h0\nwhere r(p) = rslope · p + r0\n(1)\n\n\nθ(p) = ωchroma · (p − p0 ).\nh(p), r(p), and θ(p) denote the height, radius, and phase,\nrespectively. They are linear functions of p (Fig. 4). While\nthe linear increase of h(p) and θ(p) follows Shepard’s pitch\nhelix (Fig. 2), r(p) is also defined linearly to capture the\ncone-like variations observed in our experiments. The parameters of y(p) are optimized within predefined search\nranges to minimize the mean squared error (MSE) with respect to the three-dimensional embeddings.\n\nFigure 5: Amplitude spectra for different H with a fixed\nf0 and decay tilt.\n𝑑 = decay\n\nFigure 6: Amplitude spectra for different tilt conditions\nwith a fixed f0 and H.\n\n3.4 Analysis Using Artificial Test Signals\nAs described in Sec. 2.3, the presence and amplitude of\nharmonic components in instrument sounds affect timbre.\nIn this section, we describe a methodology for investigating which signal components contribute to helix formation\nby measuring Helicality with artificial test signals.\n3.4.1 Synthesis of Harmonic-Controlled Artificial Signals\n(d)\n\nAn artificial signal sH,f0 (t) at time t is defined as:\nX (d)\n(d)\nah sin(2πf0 ht) ,\nsH,f0 (t) =\n\nTo quantify how well the three-dimensional embeddings\nalign with the helical model in Eq. (1), we apply the Helicality [19] as the inverse of the MSE. A higher score indicates a closer fit to the helical structure.\nFormally,\ngiven three-dimensional embeddings\nNkey\n{xp }p=1\n, the Helicality is defined as:\n\n−1\nNkey\nX\n1\n2\nHelicality = \n|xp − y(p)|  . (2)\nNkey p=1\n\u0001\nWe compute this score for each of the 53 projections described in Sec. 3.1, and use the maximum score as the Helicality for each layer.\n\n(3)\n\nh∈H\n\nwhere H denotes the set of harmonic indices included in\nthe signal. The fundamental component h = 1 is always\nincluded in H, while integer harmonics h = 2, 3, . . . and\nsubharmonics h = 21 , 13 , . . . are selectively included. Let\nHall denote the set of all possible harmonic indices, i.e.,\nH ⊂ Hall . The effects of different H are shown in Fig. 5.\nA variable d denotes the spectral tilt, where d ∈ D =\n{flat, decay}. To control the amplitude of each harmonic\n(d)\ncomponent, we define the amplitude coefficients ah as:\n(flat)\n\nah\n\n= 1,\n\n(4)\n\n(decay)\n\n= min(h, 1/h).\n\n(5)\n\nah\n3.3 Helicality\n\n𝑑 = flat\n\nEquation (4) corresponds to equal-amplitude harmonics,\nwhereas Eq. (5) applies symmetric amplitude decay as the\nfrequency ratio deviates from f0 (Fig. 6). These conditions\nyield different timbres for the same harmonic index h.\n3.4.2 Helicality for Each Condition\nFor each harmonic condition (H, d), we synthesize Nkey\nsignals with different pitches. We denote the set of the\n(d)\n(d)\nsignals as SH = {sH,f0 }f0 ∈F , where F is the set of fundamental frequencies corresponding to a predefined pitch\nrange. The signals are input into a target MFM, and Helicality is computed for each layer as described in Sec. 3.3.\nFor the statistical analysis, we consider two dependent\nvariables that summarize the layer-wise distribution of He(d)\n(d)\nlicality for each SH . Let Hℓ (SH ) denote the Helicality\n\n\fall ℓ\n(d)\n\n(d)\n\nypeak (SH ) = max Hℓ (SH ).\nall ℓ\n\n(7)\n\nWe refer to ysum as the layer-sum Helicality and ypeak as\nthe layer-max Helicality. “all ℓ” means these calculations\ntake into account all the Transformer layers. ysum represents the overall strength of the internal pitch helix across\nall layers, while ypeak represents the strength of the most\nprominent layer.\n\n20\n\nFrequency\n\nat layer ℓ. We then define the dependent variables as:\nX\n(d)\n(d)\nysum (SH ) =\nHℓ (SH ),\n(6)\n\nMean: 2.289\nMedian: 1.982\nRandom: 0.371\n\n10\n00\n\n2\n\n4\n6\n8\nLayer-max Helicality\n\nFigure 7: Distribution of layer-max Helicality across all\ninstruments for Jukebox. The dashed lines indicate the\nmean, median, and the reference score.\n\n4.1.2 Data\n3.4.3 Multiple Regression Analysis and Statistical Testing\nWe represent each harmonic condition (H, d) using binary\nvariables corresponding to combinations of harmonic indices and spectral tilt conditions, and analyze their relationship with Helicality via multiple regression. The binary variable is defined as:\n(\n1, if (h ∈ H ∧ d′ = d)\n(8)\nxh,d =\n0, otherwise,\nwhere d′ denotes the spectral tilt of the given condition.\nFor each dependent variable y ∈ {ysum , ypeak }, we assume the following linear model:\nX\ny = β0 +\nβh,d xh,d + ε,\n(9)\n(h,d)∈(Hall \\{1})×D\n\nwhere β0 is the intercept, βh,d is the regression coefficient\nrepresenting the contribution of harmonic h under the tilt\ncondition d, × denotes the Cartesian product, and ε is the\nerror term. The fundamental component h = 1 is excluded from the regression, so the summation is taken over\nHall \\ {1}. We construct (xh,d , y) for all harmonic conditions and estimate the coefficients βh,d using ordinary least\nsquares. We then perform an F-test to evaluate the significance of the overall regression model, followed by t-tests\nfor each coefficient to assess whether the corresponding\n(h, d) has a statistically significant effect on Helicality.\n4. EXPERIMENTS\n4.1 Experimental Setup\n4.1.1 Models\nWe analyze two trained MFMs: Jukebox and MusicGen.\nJukebox. We used the top-level decoder of the 5B model\nfrom jukemirlib [6] 1 . The Transformer decoder consists of 72 layers, and the dimensionality of the intermediate representations is 4, 800.\nMusicGen. We used the large model (3.3B) 2 . Text conditioning was set to an empty string, and the input music signals were encoded using the frozen EnCodec. MusicGen’s\nTransformer decoder has 48 layers, and the dimensionality\nof the intermediate representations is 2, 048.\n1 Available by appending a file name (e.g., 5b/vqvae.pth.tar,\n5b/prior_level_2.pth.tar) to https://openaipublic.\nazureedge.net/jukebox/models/.\n2 https://huggingface.co/facebook/\nmusicgen-large\n\nWe used two types of datasets: (i) instrument note dataset\nand (ii) artificial test signal dataset. For both datasets, 12\npitch classes spanning 3 octaves (C3–B5, Nkey = 36) were\nrendered as half notes at 120 BPM.\nInstrument note dataset. We used the notes subset of the SynTheory [7] dataset, which contains isolated musical notes from 92 instruments synthesized with\nTimGM6mb.sf2 [25], based on General MIDI [26] program numbers and instrument categories.\nArtificial test signal dataset. We synthesized artificial signals based on Eq. (3), in which harmonic components were explicitly controlled. We defined Hall as\n{ 41 , 13 , 12 , 1, . . . , 8}. Hs were set to cover possible choices\nfrom Hall , and spectral tilt conditions D = {flat, decay}\nwere applied to each H, resulting in 137 harmonic conditions in total.\nPreprocessing. Original signals were 4-second stereo audio sampled at 44.1 kHz, and they were converted to\nmonaural. For MusicGen, 4-second, 32 kHz, monaural\nformat was used. For Jukebox, the original signals were\nrepeated or trimmed to be 24 seconds.\n4.1.3 Parameter Optimization\nWe optimized the parameters of Eq. (1) using Optuna [27].\nEach optimization used more than 1, 000 trials and was\nrepeated three times with different random seeds. These\nhyperparameters were chosen based on preliminary experiments to ensure that the MSE values sufficiently converged. The same search ranges were used for all layers,\ninstruments, and models, e.g., the search range for ωchroma\nwas set to [− π2 , − π6 ] ∪ [ π6 , π2 ].\n4.2 Comparison among Instruments\nWe investigated whether MFMs embed pitch in a helical\nstructure, and if so, how the degree of Helicality varies\nacross different instruments. For each instrument, we computed Helicality at each layer from its signals, and defined\nthe layer-max Helicality as the score for that instrument.\nIn addition, we computed Helicality for three-dimensional\nuniform random variables (“Random”) as a reference. We\ngenerated random variables 100 times and computed their\nHelicality, obtaining a score of 0.371 ± 0.014. If the Helicality of a given instrument exceeds this value, we regard\nit as exhibiting a helical structure.\nFigure 7 shows a histogram of Helicality, and Fig. 8\n\n\f3.0\n\n±1 std\n\nMean Helicality\n\n2.5\n2.0\n1.5\n1.0\n\n3.592\n3.664\n3.697\n3.937\n4.081\n4.612\n5.455\n5.663\n6.054\n\n0.5\n0\n\n4\n6\n8\nLayer-max Helicality\n\n9.410\n\nFigure 8: Best / worst 10 instruments by layer-max Helicality for Jukebox.\nElectric clean guitar\n\n10\n\n20\n\n30\n\n40\n\nLayer\n\n50\n\n60\n\n70\n\nFigure 10: Mean Helicality across instruments at each\nlayer of Jukebox.\n\nOr\nga\nBa n tim Layer-max Helicality\nG ss bre\nCh uitar timbr s\nrom Pia tim es\nati no t bre\nc p im s\ne b\nB\nEn ras rcus res\nsem s sio\nble timb n\ntim res\nS\ny\nStr nt bres\ni h\nEth ng ti lea\nnic mb d\nt re\nSy imbr s\nRe nth es\ned\nPip tim pad\ne t bre\nim s\nbre\ns\n\nInstrument\n\nOcarina 0.812\nKalimba 0.887\nRecorder 0.897\nPiccolo 0.917\nAlto sax 0.948\nXylophone 0.972\n1.003\nSitar\n1.032\nMarimba\n1.055\nEnglish horn\nSynth .voice . 1.055\n..\n..\nOverdriven guitar\nChurch organ\nFretless bass\nDulcimer\nMusic box\nSlap bass 2\nHarpsichord\nPlucked electric bass\nElectric clean guitar\nHammond organ\n0\n2\n\n7.5\n5.0\n\nHammond organ\n\n2.5\n\nPC1\n\nPC4\n\nPC3\n\nPC3\n\nPC1\n\nPC2\n\nFigure 9: Jukebox embeddings for two instruments at the\nlayer and three-PC projection corresponding to each instrument’s layer-max Helicality.\nshows the best and worst 10 instruments ranked by Helicality. The model used to obtain these results is Jukebox.\nMFMs embed pitch in a helical structure. As shown\nin Fig. 7, the Helicality of all instruments exceeds that of\n“Random”. Although not shown due to space limitations,\nsimilar results are observed for MusicGen. Therefore, we\nconclude that both analyzed MFMs embed pitch in a helical structure. However, the average Helicality across all\ninstruments differs between models (Jukebox: 2.28, MusicGen: 1.16).\nThe helical structure varies depending on instrument timbre. We focus on the differences across instruments. As shown in Fig. 8, the scores vary widely, with a\nminimum of 0.812 for ocarina and a maximum of 9.410 for\nhammond organ. As shown in Fig. 7, most instruments are\ndistributed in a moderate range from 1 to 3, while a subset\nexhibits extremely high scores. Figure 9 shows examples\nof helical structures for different instruments. These examples illustrate that the helical structures differ across instruments, indicating that instrument timbre influences the\ngeometry of the internal pitch helix. For instance, while\nthe hammond organ exhibits a clear helix with one rotation\nper octave, the electric clean guitar shows a helix with two\nrotations per octave. A similar trend is observed for MusicGen. Comparing the instrument rankings as illustrated\nin Fig. 8 between Jukebox and MusicGen, we obtained a\nlinear correlation coefficient of r = 0.59. These results\nsuggest that the relative ordering of Helicality across instruments tends to be consistent across different models.\nThe prominent layer tends to appear in deeper lay-\n\nInstrument category\n\nFigure 11: Distribution of layer-max Helicality across instrument categories for Jukebox. Each point represents the\nscore of an individual instrument.\n\ners. Figure 10 shows the mean Helicality across instruments at each layer. The mean Helicality peaks in deeper\nlayers. These results suggest that abstract concepts such as\npitch are more clearly represented in deeper layers. This\ntendency is consistent with prior probing studies [7, 8],\nwhere deeper layers tend to perform better on downstream\ntasks. This suggests that Helicality may be related to\ndownstream performance.\nHelicality shows trends across instrument categories. Figure 11 shows the distribution of Helicality\nacross instrument categories based on General MIDI. Organ timbres show the highest mean with large variance.\nBass, guitar, and piano timbres exhibit moderate means\nwith similarly high variability. In contrast, brass and string\ntimbres show relatively stable distributions. These results\nsuggest that Helicality is influenced by both global tendencies corresponding to timbral characteristics of instrument\ncategories and instrument-specific acoustic factors.\n4.3 Analysis Using Artificial Test Signals\nIn Sec. 4.2, we investigated whether instrument timbre\ncontributes to the formation of the internal pitch helix. As\ndescribed in Section 2.3, instrument timbre can be characterized by the presence and amplitude of harmonic components. Based on the multiple regression analysis described in Sec. 3.4, we analyzed whether the presence of\neach harmonic component and the spectral tilt contribute\nto the clarity of the internal pitch helix. The dependent\n\n\f*\n\n**\n\nDecay\nFlat\n\n**\n\n0\n1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n\nCoefficient\n\n*\n\n0.25\n\n*\n\n*\n\n3\n\n4\n\n**\n\n5\n\n6\n\n7\n\n8\n\nCoefficient\n\n1\n\n20\n\n*\n\nJukebox: ysum\n\n*\n\n20\n\n1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n\nMusicGen: ypeak\n\n*\n\nDecay\nFlat\n\n*\n\n0.00\n\n**\n\n5\n\n**\n\n*\n\n3\n\n4\n\nHarmonic\n\n*6* 7\n\n*\n\n5\n\n8\n\n*\n\n3\n\n4\n\n5\n\n*6* 7\n\n8\n\nMusicGen: ysum\n\n*\n\n0\n\n1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n\nDecay\nFlat\n\n**\n\n0\n\nCoefficient\n\nCoefficient\n\nJukebox: ypeak\n\n**\n\n1/4 1/3 1/2 2\n\nDecay\nFlat\n*\n\n3\n\n4\n\nHarmonic\n\n**\n5\n\n* *\n*6 7 8\n\nFigure 12: Regression coefficients for Jukebox and MusicGen. Asterisks indicate statistical significance (p < 0.05).\n\nHarmonic set H\n\nSpectral tilt\n\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n\n{ 12 , 1, 2, 4, 8}\n{ 14 , 12 , 1, 2, 4, 8}\n{ 12 , 1, 2}\n{ 12 , 1, 2}\n{ 14 , 12 , 1, 2, 4, 8}\n\n.\n.\n.\n133\n134\n135\n136\n137\n\nRank\n\nPeak layer\n\nypeak\n\ndecay\ndecay\nflat\ndecay\nflat\n\n52\n51\n50\n56\n53\n\n7.744\n7.688\n6.906\n6.469\n5.097\n\n.\n.\n.\n\n.\n.\n.\n\n.\n.\n.\n\n.\n.\n.\n\n{1} (pure tone)\n{1, 6, 8}\n{1, 6, 8}\n{1, 7}\n{1, 7, 8}\n\n–\nflat\ndecay\ndecay\ndecay\n\n21\n36\n1\n0\n0\n\n0.619\n0.594\n0.593\n0.589\n0.583\n\nTable 1: Best and worst 5 examples of layer-max Helicality for artificial test signals in Jukebox.\n\nvariables were defined as ysum and ypeak .\nFigure 12 shows the regression coefficients for ysum and\nypeak in both Jukebox and MusicGen. Table 1 shows examples of layer-max Helicality obtained for each harmonic\ncondition in Jukebox. The F-tests indicate that the regression models are statistically significant for both ysum and\nypeak in both models. Therefore, we interpret the contributions of each (h, d) condition based on the corresponding\nt-tests of the regression coefficients.\nOctave-equivalent harmonics form the helix. As\nshown in Fig. 12, the {2, 4, 8}-th harmonics and the\n{1/2, 1/4} subharmonic components tend to exhibit positive coefficients, with p-values below 0.05 in most cases.\nConsistently, as seen in Tab. 1, artificial signals with high\nHelicality are composed exclusively of harmonics whose\nfrequency ratios are powers of 2. A similar trend is observed for MusicGen, with a linear correlation of r = 0.78\nbetween the models based on the Helicality rankings of\nartificial signals. These findings align with octave equivalence in human pitch perception and suggest that harmonic\ncomponents with frequency ratios that are powers of 2 play\nan important role in forming the internal pitch helix within\nMFMs. Furthermore, this interpretation is supported by\nSec. 4.2: the hammond organ, which achieves the highest Helicality, exhibits a spectrum dominated by octaveequivalent harmonics, as shown in Fig. 3.\n\nNon-octave-equivalent harmonics hinder helix formation. In contrast, the remaining {3, 5, 6, 7}-th harmonics and the 1/3 subharmonic component generally show\nnegative coefficients. These results suggest that harmonics that are not octave-equivalent inhibit the formation of\nthe helix. Indeed, the recorder, which has a low Helicality,\nexhibits a spectrum dominated by non-octave-equivalent\nharmonics, as shown in Fig. 3.\nSpectral decay facilitates helix formation. Focusing\non the spectral tilt conditions, the decay condition tends to\nshow larger coefficients than the flat condition for octaveequivalent harmonics. Compared to the flat condition with\nequal harmonic amplitudes, the decay condition, which resembles natural spectral decay, yields stronger contributions. This suggests that MFMs may form an internal pitch\nhelix more effectively from spectral distributions that resemble those of natural instrument sounds.\nSignals without harmonics do not form a helix. As\nshown in Tab. 1, pure tone exhibits low Helicality. Moreover, the ocarina, which has the lowest Helicality, behaves\nas a single-mode Helmholtz resonator [20] and lacks a\nclear harmonic series. Therefore, it can be regarded as\na condition similar to pure tone, leading to low Helicality. These observations indicate that signals without harmonic structure are less likely to form an internal pitch\nhelix, which is consistent with the findings regarding the\npresence of harmonics described above.\n5. CONCLUSION\nThis study demonstrated that the intermediate representations of two generative MFMs embed pitch information in\na helical structure. The clarity and geometry of this structure vary not only across the two analyzed models but also\nwith the acoustic properties of the input, particularly its\nharmonic composition. These results suggest that MFMs\nrepresent pitch not merely as frequency differences, but\nas a geometric structure grounded in harmonic relationships. However, whether the helical structure originates\nin the Transformer or earlier representations remains unclear. Further work should also examine its generality\nacross models and training data, as well as its relationship\nto downstream task performance.\n\n\f6. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nThis work was supported by JST FOREST Grant Number JPMJFR226V and JSPS KAKENHI Grant Number\nJP26K21256.\n7. REFERENCES\n[1] P. Dhariwal, H. Jun, C. Payne, J. W. Kim, A. Radford,\nand I. Sutskever, “Jukebox: A generative model for\nmusic,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.00341, 2020.\n[2] J. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez, “Simple and controllable music generation,” in NeurIPS, 2023.\n[3] A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel,\nM. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen,\nA. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank, “MusicLM: Generating music\nfrom text,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325, 2023.\n[4] Y. Li, R. Yuan, G. Zhang, Y. Ma, X. Chen,\nH. Yin, C. Xiao, C. Lin, A. Ragni, E. Benetos,\nN. Gyenge, R. Dannenberg, R. Liu, W. Chen, G. Xia,\nY. Shi, W. Huang, Z. Wang, Y. Guo, and J. Fu,\n“MERT: Acoustic music understanding model with\nlarge-scale self-supervised training,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2306.00107, 2023.\n[5] W.-H. Liao, Y. Takida, Y. Ikemiya, Z. Zhong, C.H. Lai, G. Fabbro, K. Shimada, K. Toyama, K. W.\nCheuk, M. A. Martínez-Ramírez, S. Takahashi, S. Uhlich, T. Akama, W. Choi, Y. Koyama, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n“Music foundation model as generic booster for music\ndownstream tasks,” TMLR, 2025.\n[6] R. Castellon, C. Donahue, and P. Liang, “Codified audio language modeling learns useful representations\nfor music information retrieval,” in ISMIR, 2021.\n\n[12] Z. Liu, O. Kitouni, N. Nolte, E. J. Michaud,\nM. Tegmark, and M. Williams, “Towards understanding grokking: An effective theory of representation\nlearning,” in NeurIPS, 2022.\n[13] B. Heinzerling and K. Inui, “Monotonic representation of numeric attributes in language models,” in ACL,\n2024, pp. 175–195.\n[14] R. Marjieh, V. Veselovsky, T. L. Griffiths, and\nI. Sucholutsky, “What is a number, that a large\nlanguage model may know it?”\narXiv preprint\narXiv:2502.01540, 2025.\n[15] F. Zhu, D. Dai, and Z. Sui, “Language models encode\nthe value of numbers linearly,” in COLING, 2025, pp.\n693–709.\n[16] G. Alain and Y. Bengio, “Understanding intermediate\nlayers using linear classifier probes,” in ICLR, 2017.\n[17] C. Papaioannou, E. Benetos, and A. Potamianos, “Universal music representations? evaluating foundation\nmodels on world music corpora,” in ISMIR, 2025.\n[18] V. Lostanlen, S. Sridhar, B. McFee, A. Farnsworth, and\nJ. P. Bello, “Learning the helix topology of musical\npitch,” in IEEE ICASSP, 2020, pp. 11–15.\n[19] S. Sridhar and V. Lostanlen, “Helicality: An isomapbased measure of octave equivalence in audio data,” in\nISMIR, 2020.\n[20] N. H. Fletcher and T. D. Rossing, The Physics of Musical Instruments, 2nd ed. Springer-Verlag, 1998.\n[21] A. van den Oord, O. Vinyals, and K. Kavukcuoglu,\n“Neural discrete representation learning,” in NeurIPS,\nvol. 30, 2017.\n[22] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit,\nL. Jones, A. N. Gomez, L. u. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin,\n“Attention is all you need,” in NeurIPS, vol. 30, 2017.\n\n[7] M. Wei, M. Freeman, C. Donahue, and C. Sun, “Do\nmusic generation models encode music theory?” in ISMIR, 2024.\n\n[23] A. Défossez, J. Copet, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “High\nFidelity Neural Audio Compression,” in ICLR, 2024.\n\n[8] W. Ma, X. Li, and G. Xia, “Do music LLMs learn symbolic concepts? a pilot study using probing and intervention,” in NeurIPS Workshop on Audio Imagination,\n2024.\n\n[24] C. Raffel, N. Shazeer, A. Roberts, K. Lee, S. Narang,\nM. Matena, Y. Zhou, W. Li, and P. J. Liu, “Exploring the limits of transfer learning with a unified textto-text transformer,” Journal of machine learning research, vol. 21, no. 140, pp. 1–67, 2020.\n\n[9] R. N. Shepard, “Geometrical approximations to the\nstructure of musical pitch,” Psychological Review,\nvol. 89, no. 4, pp. 305–333, 1982.\n\n[25] T. Brechbill, “Timidity++,” https://timbrechbill.com/\nsaxguru/Timidity.php, 2004.\n\n[10] A. Radford, J. Wu, R. Child, D. Luan, D. Amodei, and\nI. Sutskever, “Language models are unsupervised multitask learners,” OpenAI blog, vol. 1, no. 8, p. 9, 2019.\n[11] J. Engels, E. J. Michaud, I. Liao, W. Gurnee, and\nM. Tegmark, “Not all language model features are onedimensionally linear,” in ICLR, 2025.\n\n[26] “General midi system level 1,” the MIDI Manufacturers Association, Tech. Rep., 1991.\n[27] T. Akiba, S. Sano, T. Yanase, T. Ohta, and M. Koyama,\n“Optuna: A next-generation hyperparameter optimization framework,” in ACM SIGKDD, 2019, pp. 2623–\n2631.\n\n\f", "numLines": 1217, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1217}}}
